The best weapons in the game are the named ones.  They
are called artifacts.  Most of the other posts in this
thread has been about artifacts and rustproofing, so I
will offer a different tack:

In spite of being on a different track I will repeat
rustproofing.  Use/make rustproof weapons when possible.

Among non-artifact weapons, there aren't many that are
better than a long sword.  Among the one-handed ones are
the katana/samurai sword (rare) and the crysknife (must
be manufactured).  When it comes down to it, long swords
are almost as good as regular weapons get.  You should
be able to improve in long sword all the way to the end.

That means your problem is tactics not weapon strength.

Every so often you will get a message like "you feel more
confidence in your weapon skills".  ANY message you get
that isn't the expected one is an important message.
Some have a great deal of important meaning and this is
one of those important ones.  That means you will then
be able to use #enchance to increase your skills in
that weapon class (Classes can contain several weapon
types.  Long sword and katana are together, a few types
of broadsword, and so on).  With a Valkyrie seriously
consider moving your long sword skill class up from
(Valks don't start at Unskilled) Basic to Skilled and
eventually to Expert.  Use your dagger enough that you
eventually advance dagger to Expert as well.  When you
get Mjollnir advance it some as well.

Don't advance every time it's offered, though.  The
maximum XP level is 30 and each advancement takes at least
1 of those slots.  You won't be able to advance every
weapon you're interested in, so read up on the costs and
apply some strategy.  Valks do well advancing: long sword,
dagger, war hammer, twoweapon, saber and if you're chaotic
broadsword (I don't think Valkyries can start out chaotic
but I haven't checked in a long time, at least only
advance broadsword if you get Stormbringer or you have
some specific reason).

Once you've advanced both long sword and dagger to Skilled
try putting away your shield and practicing with #twoweapon.
Once you're Skilled at that you'll be a killing machine.
But do go back and eventually make it to Expert in both
long sword and dagger.  Using #enhance is a serious
strategy decision but every time you advance a weapon
you fight better in that weapon class.

Making your weapon more powerfull with enchantment.  There
is a type of scroll that makes your wielded weapon better.
It can take your +1 long sword to a blessed +2 long sword
or better.  Practically speaking it is feasible to get
weapons up to blessed rustproof +5 long sword and if you
are very careful +6.  Every number hits more often and hits
harder.  Cherish weapons with high enchantment numbers.
King Arthur had Excalibur and in the mythical world it
was only +1 yet he took a kingdom with it.

Missiles.  Daggers are for throwing in addition to wielding.
Even better, daggers stack so you can have a quiver full
of them.  Consider using "Q"uiver to make a stack ready
for throwing.  Even better even better, as you move from
Basic to Skilled to Expert you *throw more* daggers each
turn.  Triply better where thrown arrows and darts often
dissappear into the corpse, thrown daggers can always be
picked back up.  Even a very powerful monster is a lot
easier to kill when you've chopped it to bits with a flurry
of blessed rustproof +6 elven daggers as it advanced on you.
Also throw everything else from darts to rocks.  Throw
early.  Throw often.  Throw hard (enchanted weapons).
Throw fast (enhanced skill slots).


